**user**:
> How long would temperatures continue to climb, if CO2 emissions stopped today?
**assistant**:
> If **CO₂ emissions stopped today**, global surface temperature would likely **stop rising within roughly a decade or two**, then remain approximately flat for centuries—rather than continuing to climb for centuries.
> 
> Why:
> 
> - CO₂ concentrations would fall slowly, but the ocean would continue absorbing heat for a while.
> - Those effects largely offset: declining CO₂ forcing counterbalances ocean heat uptake.
> - An abrupt stop in fossil-fuel use would also remove cooling sulfate aerosols, causing some **near-term extra warming** over the first years to decades.
> - Warming from **other ongoing greenhouse-gas emissions** (especially methane and nitrous oxide) would continue unless those stopped too.
> 
> Even after temperatures stabilize, **sea level, ice loss, and some ecosystem impacts would continue for centuries or longer**, because oceans and ice sheets respond slowly.
